<p>Utilitarianism, Chapters 1 and 2</p>
<p><img src="/phil2306/assets/images/gettyimages-117455003-612x612.jpg" alt="Photograph of a two-pan balance scale." width="860" height="320" loading="lazy" class="page-hero-image"></p>
<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>In Chapter 2 Mill states his theory and answers most of the standard objections to it. This is the chapter that introduces utilitarianism to the world.</p>
</div></div>
<div class="accordion">
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">1</span>
    The Greatest Happiness Principle
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>Mill states the principle near the start of Chapter 2:</p>
<div class="callout callout--connection"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">➡️</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>"Actions are right in proportion as they tend to promote happiness, wrong as they tend to produce the reverse of happiness. By happiness is intended pleasure, and the absence of pain; by unhappiness, pain, and the privation of pleasure."</p>
</div></div>
<p>Unpack what this says. Mill is making three claims, which work together.</p>
<p>First, <strong>happiness is what morally matters</strong>. Mill defines happiness in terms of pleasure and the absence of pain, and he is going to defend the claim that this is the only thing that has intrinsic moral value. Everything else that we call good is good only in some derivative sense: courage is good because courageous people tend to lead happier lives, knowledge is good because it tends to make our pleasures richer, and so on. Happiness alone is good in itself.</p>
<p>Second, <strong>actions are right insofar as they promote happiness</strong>. The right action is the one that produces the most happiness, all things considered, across everyone affected. Notice the word "tend": Mill is not asking you to predict the future with certainty. He is asking whether the action, of its kind, generally promotes happiness or generally harms it. Mostly truth-telling promotes happiness; mostly lying harms it; we can say so without claiming to know what every particular instance of truth-telling or lying will produce.</p>
<p>Third, <strong>everyone counts equally</strong>. This is not always explicit in the principle's wording but it is essential to Mill's view. The standard is the "greatest happiness of the greatest number," which means the calculation has to weight every person's pleasure and pain the same. Your happiness counts; my happiness counts the same; the happiness of someone you have never met counts the same. This is the part of utilitarianism that makes it morally demanding and politically radical.</p>
<p>Three features of this principle are worth flagging now, because each will come up again in the chapters that follow.</p>
<p>The principle is <strong>consequentialist</strong>: what matters morally is what an action produces, not the intention behind it or the character it expresses. (Contrast Kant, for whom only the motive of duty gives an action moral worth.)</p>
<p>The principle is <strong>welfarist</strong>: the only thing that matters about consequences is whether they affect someone's well-being, understood as pleasure and pain. (Contrast accounts of value that recognize, say, knowledge or beauty or autonomy as good in themselves, regardless of whether they make anyone happier.)</p>
<p>The principle is <strong>aggregative</strong>: morality requires summing pleasures and pains across all affected persons. This will turn out to be the most controversial feature when we get to justice in Chapter 5, because aggregation seems to permit sacrificing one person's well-being for a larger gain to others.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">2</span>
    Higher and lower pleasures
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>The first objection Mill has to address is the most famous: the doctrine that pleasure is the only good is, his critics said, a doctrine "worthy only of swine." If happiness is pleasure and pleasure alone, what distinguishes a flourishing human life from a contented pig's? Both can be measured in pleasure; if utilitarianism is right, we should not prefer the human life over the pig's.</p>
<p>Mill's response, and one of the most important moves in the book, is to distinguish higher pleasures from lower pleasures. Bentham, his predecessor, had treated all pleasures as essentially the same, differing only in quantity (intensity, duration, certainty, and so on). Mill thinks this is wrong. Some pleasures are not just more <em>intense</em> than others but qualitatively <em>higher</em>: the pleasures of intellect, of friendship, of aesthetic appreciation, of moral and social engagement. These are different in kind from the pleasures of food, drink, and physical sensation, and they are worth more.</p>
<p>How do we know they are worth more? Mill's test is famously simple: ask anyone who has experienced both. He claims that almost everyone who has tasted both higher and lower pleasures will, on reflection, prefer the higher even when the higher comes with more difficulty or less intensity. From this he generates the line that is among the most quoted in all of moral philosophy: it is better to be a human being dissatisfied than a pig satisfied; better to be Socrates dissatisfied than a fool satisfied.</p>
<p>This move solves the swine objection: human happiness, properly understood, includes the higher pleasures, and the higher pleasures are what give a properly human life its value. But it raises a new question. Is Mill still a utilitarian? Bentham would say no; if some pleasures count more than others not because they are more pleasant but because of some independent feature (intellectual depth, moral seriousness, aesthetic complexity), then it is that feature, not pleasure itself, that is doing the moral work. The accusation has stuck. Many philosophers think Mill's higher-pleasures move smuggles in a non-utilitarian standard while claiming to be a more refined utilitarianism. Whether Mill can be defended on this point is one of the live debates in the secondary literature.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">3</span>
    Other objections Mill answers
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>The bulk of Chapter 2 is Mill working through a series of standard objections to utilitarianism. He takes each one seriously, partly because he thinks they can all be answered and partly because he knows his readers have absorbed the objections from the surrounding culture. Track the structure: Mill states an objection, restates it in its strongest form, and then responds.</p>
<p><strong>Happiness is unattainable.</strong> The objection: lasting happiness is impossible, so making it the goal of morality is foolish. Mill's response: utilitarianism does not require permanent ecstasy. It requires a life containing many pleasures and few pains, with reasonable expectations about both. Most people are capable of such a life if their basic needs are met and they have not been raised badly.</p>
<p><strong>Doing without happiness is a virtue.</strong> The objection: history is full of moral heroes who sacrificed their own happiness for others, and we admire them for it. Doesn't this show that happiness is not the highest good? Mill's response: the sacrifice was for the happiness of others, which is itself the utilitarian end. Self-sacrifice is not valued for its own sake but because it produces more total happiness than self-protection would.</p>
<p><strong>Utilitarianism makes morality cold and calculating.</strong> The objection: requiring us to calculate consequences before every action turns moral life into a spreadsheet. Mill's response: this misunderstands how moral rules work. Most of the time we follow inherited rules (don't lie, don't steal, keep your promises) precisely because past experience has shown that these rules tend to maximize happiness. We only need direct calculation in unusual cases where the rules conflict or fail. This is the move that later commentators have called <em>rule utilitarianism</em>; the everyday rules track utility, and we use them as proxies rather than calculating from scratch each time.</p>
<p><strong>We cannot predict consequences.</strong> The objection: we don't know what an action will actually produce; how can the rightness of an action depend on facts we cannot know? Mill's response: we know enough. Most actions have predictable enough consequences for us to act sensibly. Where we genuinely cannot predict, the action does not become wrong but unknowable, and we do the best we can with the information available.</p>
<p><strong>Motivations matter, not consequences.</strong> The objection: surely a virtuous motive is what makes an action good. Mill's response: motive matters for evaluating the agent, but not for evaluating the action. A drowning child rescued by someone who wanted publicity is just as rescued as one saved by a saint. The morally important question is whether the child was saved.</p>
<p>Each of these answers can be challenged, and you should challenge them where you can. Mill is making a series of moves, and one of the goals of this lecture is for you to see where the moves are, so you can take a stance on whether they succeed.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">4</span>
    Act and rule utilitarianism
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>Mill does not use the terms "act utilitarianism" and "rule utilitarianism"; those came later. But the distinction is already present in Chapter 2, and you will need both terms for the reflection prompt at the end of this unit.</p>
<p>An <strong>act utilitarian</strong> applies the principle of utility directly to each particular action. The right action, on this view, is whichever specific action (in these specific circumstances, with these specific consequences) produces the most happiness. If lying in some particular case would maximize happiness, the act utilitarian says: lie.</p>
<p>A <strong>rule utilitarian</strong> applies the principle of utility to general rules of conduct, not to individual actions. The right action is the one that conforms to the rule that, if generally followed, would produce the most happiness. So even if lying in some particular case would maximize happiness in that case, the rule utilitarian says: don't lie, because the general practice of truth-telling is what produces happiness over time. Individual exceptions, even when they look like they would do more good, erode the rule and produce more harm in the long run.</p>
<p>The distinction matters because it generates different answers in the cases where moral intuitions diverge most sharply from raw utility calculations. Consider the famous "innocent scapegoat" case: a sheriff in a Southern town can prevent a mob from killing many innocent people by framing one innocent person for a crime they did not commit. The hanging of the scapegoat would produce more happiness (one death prevents many) than the failure to frame would. An act utilitarian seems committed to recommending the frame-up. A rule utilitarian can resist: the general practice of framing innocent people, even when it appears locally beneficial, would erode trust in justice in ways that produce far more harm than the immediate case shows.</p>
<p>Mill is closer to the rule utilitarian position. Throughout Chapter 2 he insists that secondary moral rules (don't lie, don't steal, keep your promises) are not optional approximations to be set aside when calculation suggests otherwise. They are the accumulated moral wisdom of the human race, and the principle of utility justifies following them rather than recalculating from scratch in every case. But he is not a pure rule utilitarian: in genuine emergencies, when the rules conflict or are obviously inapplicable, he thinks we go back to the principle of utility directly.</p>
<p>The unit’s reflection prompt asks you to apply both an act and a rule utilitarian framework to a concrete case and to defend one approach over the other. Make sure the distinction is clear before you get there.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
